# Can a rolling spend cap bound a Blend-shaped SUPPLY, and can the agent dodge it?
# Generated 2026-08-31T17:20:00Z by packages/policy-synth/scripts/oz-two-rule-blend-cap.ts
#
# `oz-spending-limit-binding.log` settled that an OZ spending_limit binds a
# DIRECT `transfer` sharing a rule with our interpreter. It could not answer the
# question people actually ask, because a supply is not a transfer: the built-in
# refuses any call not named `transfer` (spending_limit.rs:242 at tag v0.7.2), so
# a spend cap CANNOT sit on the pool's rule at all.
#
# What makes a supply cappable is that it is not ONE authorization context.
# Decoding the two mainnet Blend envelopes in the replay corpus shows the shape:
#
#   - CAJJZSGMMM… submit(from, spender, to, requests)
#       - CDTKPWPLOU… transfer(from, pool, 58929517)
#
# `do_check_auth` validates each context against its OWN declared rule and then
# enforces that rule's policies against that context, so the cap goes on a
# second, token-scoped rule and meters the nested leg.
#
# Three rules, one agent. The declared ids are the only variable:
#   rule 1  POOL     CallContract(pool)  interpreter (submit shape + per-entry)
#   rule 2  CAPPED   CallContract(SAC)   interpreter + spending_limit(cap 5000000)
#   rule 3  CONTROL  CallContract(SAC)   interpreter only, NO cap
#
# The per-call ceiling in BOTH predicates is 100000000, far above the over-cap
# amount. Had a predicate refused first the deny would carry #100 and would say
# nothing about the cap; this way the rolling total is the only bound that can
# refuse the over-cap call.
#
# Five calls, each load-bearing:
#   1. CONTROL over-cap supply, ids [1,3] -> must PASS (attributes any later
#      deny to the cap alone rather than to a broke account or a bad request)
#   2. CAPPED under-cap, ids [1,2]        -> must PASS (a bound, not a brick)
#   3. CAPPED over-cap,  ids [1,2]        -> must DENY #3221 SpendingLimitExceeded
#   4. BYPASS ids [1,1]                   -> must DENY #3002 UnvalidatedContext
#      The "just name the pool rule for the token leg" dodge.
#   5. BYPASS ids [1]                     -> must DENY #3014 ContextRuleIdsLengthMismatch
#      Whether a context can be dropped so the token leg goes unjudged.
#
# Note what (1) also demonstrates on purpose: rule 3 is a live bypass of rule 2's
# cap for the SAME agent. That is R-4 cross-rule authority, in one run. The cap
# binds the rule, never the key.
#
# The spending_limit contract is OZ example code we built from tag v0.7.2 and
# deployed ourselves. We did not audit it; see docs/audit/README.md.

$ bun packages/policy-synth/scripts/oz-two-rule-blend-cap.ts --network testnet
