# ---- WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW ----
#
# The pool is `contracts/test-blend-pool`, a stub whose `submit` pulls each
# request's token so the auth tree matches a real Blend supply. It is not Blend:
# no reserve accounting, no bTokens, no withdraw. What is evidenced is the AUTH
# SHAPE and the policy composition over it, not Blend's economics.
#
# Cases 4 and 5 returned DIFFERENT codes (#3002 vs #3014) through the same
# assertion path, so the matcher discriminates rather than accepting any failure.
#
# Not evidenced here: that rule 2 is the agent's only route to that token. Rule 3
# exists in this very run and is exactly such a route. `install_policy`'s
# authority scan refuses only an UNPOLICED neighbour, and it does not compare cap
# amounts, so a second capped rule on the same token is reported as advisory at
# most. The cap binds the rule, not the key.
